// Every GLB in the kit, shaded like everything else in the scene.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. gltfio ships two material providers, and both were wrong for
// this game. The JIT one composes GLSL at run time and needs libfilamat linked
// into the wasm; the ubershader one (what we used) is a handful of pre-built
// materials whose fragment shader carries the WHOLE glTF surface model —
// metallic-roughness, clear coat, sheen, transmission, volume, specular — behind
// uniform branches, with four or five samplers bound per instance whether the
// asset uses them or not.
//
// The kit uses none of it. Every GLB under public/assets/toycar is one of three
// shapes: a base-colour texture (the shared colormap atlas) with metallic 0, or a
// flat baseColorFactor with no texture at all, and all of them OPAQUE. So the
// ubershader was a PBR evaluation and four dead sampler binds per draw, in a
// scene where nothing else is lit that way at all — the road, the hills, the
// berms, the structures and the scenery meshes all run the matte Lambert in
// ttp_shade.inc, and the cars, props and landmark GLBs were the one family
// shaded by a different model. Measured at four players, the GLBs were 37% of
// the frame's draw calls and 44% of its texture binds.
//
// This is that same matte shading with a base-colour texture in front of it. One
// program for the whole kit, ONE sampler.
//
// WHAT IT BOUGHT, measured at four players on a 3200x1800 canvas, on the
// countdown grid (a real race frame that holds still, so the reading is not
// swamped by where an unseeded race happened to point the cameras): texture binds
// 243 -> 97 per frame, CPU 3.40 -> 1.90 ms, uncapped throughput 295 -> 318 fps.
// Draw calls, triangles and render passes did not move at all, and NEITHER DID
// GPU TIME (2.87 -> 2.97, inside a baseline that itself ranged 2.79..3.14). That
// is the same answer this renderer keeps giving and it is worth not
// re-discovering: the frame is SUBMISSION-bound. What a sampler costs here is
// Filament's per-draw descriptor work on the CPU, not bandwidth on the GPU —
// which is why dropping the sun-map sampler (see TTP_NO_SUN_SHADOW) was worth
// more than the whole PBR-to-Lambert switch, and why the win is bigger on a
// weak-CPU TV box than these numbers suggest.
//
// MEASURE THIS ONE WITH THE FRAME-RATE LIMIT OFF. Under vsync the GPU is idle
// four fifths of every frame, downclocks, and an identical build reads anywhere
// across a 1.5 ms band: the first pass at this change read as a 35% REGRESSION
// that way round, and the interleaved uncapped run showed the opposite.
//
// WHAT IT IS NOT. This does not replace gltfio's provider — TtpGlbMaterials (see
// TtpRenderer.cpp) hands anything this material cannot express BACK to an
// ubershader provider it keeps beside it: a blend or mask alpha mode, a normal /
// metallic-roughness / emissive / occlusion map, a uv transform, or a base colour
// on UV1. Nothing the kit ships takes that path except the GHOST twins
// (glb.cc rewrites them to alphaMode BLEND), which are four assets that only
// enter the scene while a monster truck is blocking someone's view.
//
// THE PARAMETER NAMES ARE A CONTRACT, not a choice. gltfio's AssetLoader fills
// SIX of these BY NAME on every instance it creates, whatever the glTF material
// actually carries — baseColorFactor, emissiveFactor, metallicFactor,
// roughnessFactor, and (from the else branch of a texture test, which is easy to
// miss when reading it) normalScale and aoStrength. Filament PANICS on
// setParameter for a name the material does not declare, so all six are here.
// Four are dead: this model has no metal, no roughness, no normal map and no
// emissive. Declaring them is also what keeps TtpRenderer's own scenery pass —
// which forces metallicFactor 0 / roughnessFactor 1 on every scenery instance —
// working unchanged against either provider.
//
// baseColorUvMatrix is the same contract with a twist: gltfio sets it only when
// the source declares KHR_texture_transform, and EVERY textured GLB in this kit
// does, with an empty transform that names its own texcoord set and nothing
// else. So the matrix is the identity in practice and the multiply below costs
// six madds — but a provider that refused the flag outright (the first cut of
// this) would have handed the WHOLE kit back to the ubershader and changed
// nothing at all.
material {
    name : TtpVglb,
    // The per-vertex fog factor (ttp_fog.inc); .yzw unused.
    variables : [ fog ],
    requires : [
        // the base colour atlas coordinate
        uv0,
        // the world normal the Lambert term needs (getWorldGeometricNormalVector)
        tangents
    ],
    shadingModel : unlit,
    // Declared two-sided so gltfio's per-instance setDoubleSided/setCullingMode
    // can honour each glTF material's own flag — the kit's textured props are
    // doubleSided, its palm and cactus foliage is not, and a ghost twin is
    // explicitly single-sided so its 50% alpha is not laid over itself.
    culling : none,
    doubleSided : true,
    // glTF uv0 is a real image coordinate and gltfio hands it over unflipped —
    // base.mat.in (its own ubershader) says flipUV:false for the same reason.
    // Left at Filament's default every kit texture samples upside down, which on
    // an atlas is not a flip but a WRONG TILE: the cars come out wearing another
    // car's paint band.
    flipUV : false,
    // NO SHADOW PARAMETERS, and none of ttp_shade.inc's sun lookup either — see
    // TTP_NO_SUN_SHADOW there for why a kit GLB is not a shadow receiver.
    parameters : [
        // — what gltfio fills by name (see the header) —
        { type : sampler2d, name : baseColorMap },
        { type : mat3, name : baseColorUvMatrix, precision : high },
        { type : float4, name : baseColorFactor },
        { type : float3, name : emissiveFactor },
        { type : float, name : metallicFactor },
        { type : float, name : roughnessFactor },
        { type : float, name : normalScale },
        { type : float, name : aoStrength },
        { type : sampler2d, name : gradeLut }
    ]
}

fragment {
    #define TTP_NO_SUN_SHADOW
    #include "ttp_shade.inc"
    #include "ttp_grade.inc"
    #include "ttp_fog.inc"
    #include "ttp_glb.inc"

    void material(inout MaterialInputs material) {
        prepareMaterial(material);
        // Unlit adds `emissive` to `baseColor`; all the shading goes in the
        // former so nothing else in the pipeline touches it.
        material.baseColor = vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
        // emissive.w = 0 → no second exposure; both terms are pre-exposed.
        material.emissive = vec4(ttpFogMix(ttpGrade(ttpMatteShade(
                ttpGlbBaseColor().rgb, getWorldGeometricNormalVector())), variable_fog.x), 0.0);
    }
}

vertex {
    #include "ttp_fog.inc"

    // The fog factor is the only thing this stage exists for: per vertex, so the
    // fragment pays a mix instead of a sqrt and an exp. See ttp_fog.inc.
    void materialVertex(inout MaterialVertexInputs material) {
        material.fog = vec4(ttpFogFactor(material.worldPosition.xyz), 0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
    }
}
